<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Ziyarid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">931 CE – 1090 CE (approx.) · capital Gorgan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Gilite dynasty of the Caspian lands whose founder dreamed of restoring the Persian empire and whose prince Qabus built the soaring tower of Gonbad-e Qabus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij ibn Ziyar</a>, a Gilite commander who had served Daylamite warlords, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> around 931. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, celebrated old Iranian festivals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadeh">Sadeh</a> and was said to plan the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and a revived Sasanian monarchy. His Turkish slave guards murdered him in 935.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vushmgir">Vushmgir</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>, once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij's</a> officers, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus">Qabus ibn Vushmgir</a>, ruler in 977 to 981 and 997 to 1012, was a learned prince and stylist in Arabic, patron of al-Biruni, and built the brick tomb tower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonbad-e-qabus-tower">Gonbad-e Qabus</a> in 1006 to 1007. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keikavus">Keikavus</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus-nama">Qabus-nama</a>, a classic Persian book of advice for princes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Ziyarids became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. The dynasty faded around 1090, when its last prince, Gilanshah, lost his lands, reportedly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari Ismailis</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ziyarid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ziyarid dynasty" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab_Hafez (Uploads) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziyardi_Dynasty_928_-_1043_(AD).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>931 CE – 1090 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gorgan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mardavij, Qabus, Keikavus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ziyarids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1010&amp;focus=ziyarid-dynasty" aria-label="Ziyarid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6006 991 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ziyarid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6326 1247L6588 1010M6326 1247L6748 1217M6326 1247L6481 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6588 1010h0M6748 1217h0M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1010&amp;focus=ziyarid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

